  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia has supported strengthening Pacific experience and expertise through capacity building activities for Pacific emergency medical teams (EMTs). Supported by DFAT funded programs, Pacific countries are providing significant contributions to global EMT lessons-learned processes by sharing their extensive knowledge and crisis response experience with international audiences. 
&#x200d;
 During 2024/25, experiences and lessons from Pacific EMT were shared through 21 regional and international publications and presentations (from a baseline of 0). This was achieved through the support of the World Health Organization (WHO). Australia supported the attendance of Pacific EMT representatives at the 2024 WHO Global EMT meeting, both as participants and presenters. The WHO reported that colleagues from Africa and the Middle East were impressed by Pacific representation and engagement at this global meeting. 
&#x200d;
 The investment has resulted in a significant shift in sharing lessons and experiences and has strengthened Pacific voices within the global EMT community.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s support for capacity building of Pacific emergency medical teams (EMTs) has been successful in strengthening local leadership and capability. This work has led to six EMTs being recognised by the World Health Organization as national EMTs. Over the past four years, these EMTs have also successfully led responses to 12 national emergencies without needing international assistance. 
&#x200d;
Through Australia&apos;s support, the number of local instructors in the Pacific region and Bali doubled from 13 in 2017 to 29 in 2022. These instructors have now delivered 17 of the 20 major incident medical management training activities in the Pacific since 2017.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Samoa Emergency Medical Assistance Team (SEMAT) team, supported by Australia, through the World Health Organization and an Australian Medical Assistance Team leader, undertook their first team member training. The first cohort of trained SEMAT team members includes personnel from Samoa&#x2019;s Ministry of Health, Fire and Emergency Services Authority (FESA) and the Ministry of Police, Prisons and Corrections Services. Building on work over a number of years, the SEMAT team undertook technical training, tested their draft standard operating procedures undertook four days training and undertook a simulation of cyclone scenario which tested the deployment of operating mobile clinics.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
